# Test: policy-routing tcp-mss 1400 clamps the MSS of a forwarded SYN and
#       never raises it.
# VALIDATES: spec-home-cpe-21 AC-4 -- through the daemon, a SYN arriving on the
#            policy's interface with MSS 1460 leaves with 1400, one with 1300
#            leaves with 1300, and one with no MSS option leaves with none and
#            a 20-octet TCP header, for IPv4 and IPv6.
# PREVENTS: a policy tcp-mss that raises a smaller MSS. RFC 9293 Section
#           3.7.2 makes the MSS an upper bound the receiver chose.
#
# policy-tcp-mss.ci proves the rule is in the ruleset. This test proves what
# the rule does to a packet: the fixture (policyTCPMSSClamp,
# internal/test/fixture/netfilter_fixture_policy_mss.go) waits for the ze_pr
# rule, builds a router in this test's namespace with internal/test/msslab
# (veth zemssin carries the SYN in, veth zemssout carries it out), injects
# each SYN on zemssinp and reads the forwarded one on zemssoutp.
#
# The path: config -> policyroute buildActions (internal/plugins/policyroute/
# translate.go) appends firewall.SetTCPMSS -> lowerSetTCPMSS
# (internal/plugins/firewall/nft/lower_linux.go) -> the kernel's two-byte
# exthdr write, which skips a value already at or below 1400.
#
# The rule has no terminal action, so it sets no fwmark and the SYN keeps the
# main table, where the lab's host route forwards it out of zemssout.
#
# In a QEMU guest this test runs in the guest's root namespace, which keeps
# the devices and routes earlier suites left behind (a leftover zentk holding
# 198.51.100.2/24 once took the probe away from zemssout). msslab therefore
# pins .10 and .20 with host routes and refuses to probe unless
# `ip route get` sends the SYN out of zemssout.
#
# Breaks that turn it red, each made in Ze's code:
#   - buildActions drops the SetTCPMSS action: the 1460 SYN leaves as 1460
#     (the ze_pr wait fails first, as the rule no longer carries maxseg).
#   - lowerSetTCPMSS writes four bytes {2, 4, hi, lo} at option offset 0: the
#     write takes the unguarded arm of nft_exthdr_tcp_set_eval and the 1300
#     SYN leaves as 1400.
#
# The interface needs no option=netns-link: the fixture creates it, and an
# iifname match compares packet metadata.
#
# Requires CAP_NET_ADMIN.
#
# ISOLATION: read the header of policy-boot-apply.ci. Every test in test/policy/
# writes the same `inet ze_pr` table and the same global ip-rule table, so the
# suite runs serial with a per-test namespace. Do NOT raise its parallelism.

option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
option=timeout:value=40s

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
firewall {
	backend nft;
}
policy {
	route mss-clamp {
		interface zemssin;
		rule clamp {
			from {
				protocol tcp;
				tcp-flags syn;
			}
			then {
				tcp-mss 1400;
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# The key is DOTTED: policy-boot-apply.ci carries the explanation.
option=env:var=ze.log.policy.routes:value=info
option=env:var=ze.log.firewall:value=info

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp
expect=stderr:contains=policy routes applied
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=le test fixture policy/policy-tcp-mss-clamp:timeout=30s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=mss ipv4 sent=1460 forwarded=1400 header=24
expect=stdout:contains=mss ipv4 sent=1300 forwarded=1300 header=24
expect=stdout:contains=mss ipv4 sent=none forwarded=none header=20
expect=stdout:contains=mss ipv6 sent=1460 forwarded=1400 header=24
expect=stdout:contains=mss ipv6 sent=1300 forwarded=1300 header=24
expect=stdout:contains=mss ipv6 sent=none forwarded=none header=20
